==========================================================================
LAYER 1.  R_k = R_(d-k) for all k   <=>   roots reciprocal-closed up to scale
==========================================================================

  (<=) reciprocal-closed families must be ratio-palindromic:
    balanced two-cluster (1,2)^4       reciprocal-closed=True  ratio-palindromic=True
    balanced two-cluster (3,7)^3       reciprocal-closed=True  ratio-palindromic=True
    geometric 2^-i, d=7                reciprocal-closed=True  ratio-palindromic=True
    mirror pair set {2,3,1/2�6,1/3�6}  reciprocal-closed=True  ratio-palindromic=True
    scaled mirror {1,4,,}              reciprocal-closed=True  ratio-palindromic=True

  (=>) EXHAUSTIVE SEARCH for a ratio-palindromic set that is NOT
       reciprocal-closed (small rational root multisets):
    tested 7722 multisets (d=4,5,6 over a 10-element rational pool)
    counterexamples found: 0   NONE -- rigidity holds

  numeric confirmation of the PROOF's intermediate identity
    e_k * e_d == mu^k * e_(d-k)   with mu = e_d^(2/d):
    balanced two-cluster (1,2)^4       max|e_k e_d - mu^k e_(d-k)| = 0.000e+00
    balanced two-cluster (3,7)^3       max|e_k e_d - mu^k e_(d-k)| = 1.043e-07
    geometric 2^-i, d=7                max|e_k e_d - mu^k e_(d-k)| = 3.176e-22

  VERDICT LAYER 1: RIGIDITY CONFIRMED

==========================================================================
LAYER 2.  reversal = ANTIPODAL MAP;  c(-ell) = -reverse(c(ell))
==========================================================================
  equivariance (*), random configurations:
    d=  5  max|c(-ell) + reverse(c(ell))| = 6.661e-16
    d=  6  max|c(-ell) + reverse(c(ell))| = 6.661e-16
    d=  7  max|c(-ell) + reverse(c(ell))| = 1.554e-15
    d=  8  max|c(-ell) + reverse(c(ell))| = 4.441e-15
    d= 12  max|c(-ell) + reverse(c(ell))| = 5.773e-15

  (2a) BALANCED-LOCUS CROSSING.  Phi := sum_k c_k = log(R_(d-1)/R_1) is
       an ODD function of ell (w = 1 is reversal-symmetric), so ANY path
       from ell to -ell must cross Phi = 0.  The straight path is a
       degenerate witness (it passes through ell = 0, all roots equal),
       so the real test uses a GREAT-CIRCLE path of constant norm that
       never degenerates:  ell(t) = cos(pi t) ell + sin(pi t) ell_perp.
    d=  6  |ell(t)| constant to 2.22e-16 (never degenerates: True)
          Phi(0)=-0.010016  Phi(1)=+0.010016  opposite signs: True
          crossing found in sub-interval(s) [3]   (NOT at the degenerate center)
    d=  9  |ell(t)| constant to 4.44e-16 (never degenerates: True)
          Phi(0)=-0.142156  Phi(1)=+0.142156  opposite signs: True
          crossing found in sub-interval(s) [1, 5, 7]   (NOT at the degenerate center)
    d= 14  |ell(t)| constant to 8.88e-16 (never degenerates: True)
          Phi(0)=+0.001071  Phi(1)=-0.001071  opposite signs: True
          crossing found in sub-interval(s) [2]   (NOT at the degenerate center)

  (2b) BORSUK-ULAM.  S c : S^(d-2) -> R^ceil((d-2)/2) is odd, so it has
       a zero.  Sphere dim d-2 vs target dim ceil((d-2)/2):
    d=  4  sphere dim   2  target dim   1  BU applies: True   identified zero-set dim   1   log-symmetric locus dim in the sphere   1
    d=  5  sphere dim   3  target dim   2  BU applies: True   identified zero-set dim   1   log-symmetric locus dim in the sphere   1
    d=  6  sphere dim   4  target dim   2  BU applies: True   identified zero-set dim   2   log-symmetric locus dim in the sphere   2
    d=  7  sphere dim   5  target dim   3  BU applies: True   identified zero-set dim   2   log-symmetric locus dim in the sphere   2
    d=  8  sphere dim   6  target dim   3  BU applies: True   identified zero-set dim   3   log-symmetric locus dim in the sphere   3
    d=  9  sphere dim   7  target dim   4  BU applies: True   identified zero-set dim   3   log-symmetric locus dim in the sphere   3
    d= 10  sphere dim   8  target dim   4  BU applies: True   identified zero-set dim   4   log-symmetric locus dim in the sphere   4
    d= 11  sphere dim   9  target dim   5  BU applies: True   identified zero-set dim   4   log-symmetric locus dim in the sphere   4
    d= 12  sphere dim  10  target dim   5  BU applies: True   identified zero-set dim   5   log-symmetric locus dim in the sphere   5
       (the two dimensions agree, which is Layer 1: the BU zero set IS
        the log-symmetric locus, nothing more and nothing less.)
  VERDICT LAYER 2: EQUIVARIANCE + CROSSING CONFIRMED

==========================================================================
LAYER 3.  MULTIPOLE DICTIONARY AND THE SPREAD CRITERION
==========================================================================
  (i) jets are additive over factorisation  (= FMM superposition):
    trial 0: p_j(F*G) == p_j(F) + p_j(G) for j=1..5 : True
    trial 1: p_j(F*G) == p_j(F) + p_j(G) for j=1..5 : True
    trial 2: p_j(F*G) == p_j(F) + p_j(G) for j=1..5 : True
    => THM-2997 (21)'s wall subtraction A=P_1-w_1 D^2 etc. is exactly
       multipole subtraction; w_j are the WALL's multipole moments.

  (ii) CANCELLATION-AWARE SPREAD CRITERION.
       kappa=max|r|/m1, chi=mean|r|/m1, 1<=chi<=kappa, and exactly
            |m_j/m_1^j| <= chi*kappa^(j-1) <= kappa^j.
       THM-3000's remainder range is 4<=j<=k+1.  Since j=4 binds:
         arbitrary phases:          kappa=o(d^(1/4))
         bounded chi / r_i>0:       kappa=o(d^(1/3))
         chi=O(kappa^theta):         kappa=o(d^(1/(3+theta))).
       edge k : required arbitrary-phase exponent
         k=2:  no j>=4 jet enters; curvature alone
         k=3:  kappa = o(d^0.2500)   (4<=j<=k+1, binding at j=4)
         k=4:  kappa = o(d^0.2500)   (4<=j<=k+1, binding at j=4)
         k=5:  kappa = o(d^0.2500)   (4<=j<=k+1, binding at j=4)
         k=6:  kappa = o(d^0.2500)   (4<=j<=k+1, binding at j=4)
         k=7:  kappa = o(d^0.2500)   (4<=j<=k+1, binding at j=4)
         k=8:  kappa = o(d^0.2500)   (4<=j<=k+1, binding at j=4)

  (iii) numeric check of |m_j/m_1^j| <= chi*kappa^(j-1) <= kappa^j:
    uniform i/d, d=60      kappa=  1.9672 chi=  1.0000
        j=2: |q_j|=     1.32240 <= chi*kappa^(j-1)=       1.96721 <= kappa^j=       3.86993  True
        j=3: |q_j|=     1.96721 <= chi*kappa^(j-1)=       3.86993 <= kappa^j=       7.61297  True
        j=4: |q_j|=     3.12146 <= chi*kappa^(j-1)=       7.61297 <= kappa^j=      14.97634  True
        j=5: |q_j|=     5.15920 <= chi*kappa^(j-1)=      14.97634 <= kappa^j=      29.46165  True
    two-cluster 1,2 d=60   kappa=  1.3333 chi=  1.0000
        j=2: |q_j|=     1.11111 <= chi*kappa^(j-1)=       1.33333 <= kappa^j=       1.77778  True
        j=3: |q_j|=     1.33333 <= chi*kappa^(j-1)=       1.77778 <= kappa^j=       2.37037  True
        j=4: |q_j|=     1.67901 <= chi*kappa^(j-1)=       2.37037 <= kappa^j=       3.16049  True
        j=5: |q_j|=     2.17284 <= chi*kappa^(j-1)=       3.16049 <= kappa^j=       4.21399  True
    one heavy root d=60    kappa= 30.2521 chi=  1.0000
        j=2: |q_j|=    15.50314 <= chi*kappa^(j-1)=      30.25210 <= kappa^j=     915.18961  True
        j=3: |q_j|=   461.56618 <= chi*kappa^(j-1)=     915.18961 <= kappa^j=   27686.40823  True
        j=4: |q_j|= 13959.59711 <= chi*kappa^(j-1)=   27686.40823 <= kappa^j=  837572.01356  True
        j=5: |q_j|=422305.24897 <= chi*kappa^(j-1)=  837572.01356 <= kappa^j=25338313.01540  True

  (iv) EXACT OLD-EXPONENT HOSTILE WITH ALL POLYNOMIAL COEFFICIENTS POSITIVE:
       N_n(t)=((t+1)^2+n^6)^(n^s), roots r=1+/-i*n^3.
       m1=1, kappa=chi=sqrt(1+n^6), q4=1-6n^6+n^12.
    n= 2: kappa/d10^(2/5)=0.381878, q4/d10=1.812988, q4/d12=0.453247
    n= 3: kappa/d10^(2/5)=0.252793, q4/d10=4.462971, q4/d12=0.495886
    n= 5: kappa/d10^(2/5)=0.151577, q4/d10=12.495200, q4/d12=0.499808
    n=10: kappa/d10^(2/5)=0.075786, q4/d10=49.999700, q4/d12=0.499997
       first ratio ->0 but q4/d10 -> infinity; at s=12 q4/d ->1/2.
       old k-dependent exponent fails; the general 1/4 boundary is attained.

  (v) FIRST-GAP CONSEQUENCE.  THM-2997 (24) gives d/M -> 62/3 and
       u/M^2 -> 131/12, so mean root -> (131/12)/(62/3) M = 0.5282 M.
       mean root  ~ 0.528226 M ,  d ~ 20.6667 M
       every fixed edge needs only kappa = o(d^(1/4)), i.e.
           max|r| = o(0.5282 * (20.667 M)^(1/4) * M) = o(1.1263 M^(5/4)).
       So a root-modulus bound |r| = o(M^(5/4)) on the wall-stripped core
       CLOSES EVERY FIXED EDGE -- not uniformly when k grows with d.
       If chi is bounded, max|r|=o(M^(4/3)) is enough instead.
  VERDICT LAYER 3: DICTIONARY + CRITERION CONFIRMED

